add('def-root-height-completion-for-weightwise-quasi-r-intertwiners',[
('zero',C,'The root-degree component at the origin is included in the diagonal product, and the prescribed quasi-intertwiner normalization uses the identity there. A zero family has every component zero and acts by the zero operator wherever the stated finite-tail condition holds.','Definition paragraphs on diagonal product, componentwise multiplication and identity normalization'),
('one',C,'The identity family has only its root-origin component nonzero. Its convolution with any diagonal family retains each component, and its action is a finite sum without needing an additional tail argument. The prescribed normalization therefore has a meaningful unit component.','Definition componentwise multiplication and final normalization paragraph'),
('degenerate',C,'If either tensor factor is the zero module, every homogeneous action term vanishes. Its support is empty and the stated tail-vanishing condition holds immediately, so the coefficientwise action reduces to the zero action on the zero tensor space.','Definition finite action paragraph'),
('endpoints',NA,'The support bounds here decide whether a completed family can act, rather than describing a limiting value of the formal parameter. The Definition explicitly withholds evaluation on a two-sided unbounded tensor pair lacking tail vanishing, so it does not assert convergence at a missing support endpoint.','Definition finite action paragraph and Remarks'),
('iff-reverse',NA,'The completed tensor family and its admissible action are prescribed structures. No claim recovers bounded module support from the existence of an action, and the Definition asserts neither a universal braiding nor a converse characterization of quasi-intertwiners.','Definition support condition and exclusions'),
('nonempty-choice',NA,'Diagonal components are taken over the supplied positive root monoid, and convolution sums range over all root decompositions of a fixed degree. They do not select representatives or dual bases. The finite-tail action is part of the prescribed support condition, not a selection from nonempty sets.','Definition diagonal product and action; no choice declaration here'),
('iff-forward',NA,'Specifying the positive-first tensor orientation, coefficientwise convolution and a finite-tail action defines the ambient completion. It does not claim that every family in that completion is an intertwiner or satisfies an equivalence criterion.','Definition normalization transport and exclusions')])
