add('lem-cyclotomic-jantzen-equivalence-is-schur-and-hecke-block-linkage',[
('one',NA,'The number of cyclotomic charges is allowed to be any positive level. The argument depends on the finite multipartition dominance order and the standard-class basis, rather than a special singleton charge calculation, so the same linkage argument applies at the lowest level.','Statement level range; Proof1.1–3.1'),
('nonempty-choice',C,'A nonzero coefficient in a finite integer sum supplies a multipartition with a nonzero connecting term. Iterating this finite dominance descent builds the required chain without an arbitrary-index selection or a new choice axiom.','Proof2.2; Remarks finite chains'),
('iff-forward',C,'A nonzero Jantzen coefficient cannot cross a primitive central block: projection of the sum formula and independence of standard classes force the outside-block coefficient to vanish. Every generated Jantzen chain therefore stays within a Schur block, and the supplied Schur–Specht correspondence gives the Hecke block implication.','Proof2.1 and3.1'),
('iff-reverse',C,'Standards in a common Schur block are connected by a finite shared-simple-factor chain. The strictly decreasing dominance argument turns each shared factor into Jantzen connections, and the Schur–Specht block correspondence transfers the same reverse implication from the Hecke description.','Proof2.2 and3.1'),
('endpoints',NA,'The parameter is fixed at a nontrivial root of unity and the multipartition rank is fixed during the argument. Termination uses a finite dominance poset; there is no limiting parameter approach or infinite-rank endpoint assertion.','Statement parameter restrictions; Proof2.2'),
('degenerate',NA,'The Hecke parameter equal to the multiplicative identity is excluded by the Statement, as is root order below the stated range. This lemma proves the nondegenerate cyclotomic block-linkage equivalence and does not transfer it to the separate degenerate Hecke presentation.','Statement parameter range'),
('zero',C,'At rank zero there is one empty standard. Its positive Jantzen filtration terms vanish, and its sole index is a singleton equivalence class, as the final proof paragraph explicitly states.','Statement final rank clause; Proof3.1'),
('empty',C,'The empty multipartition is the unique rank-zero label, not an absent block. The standard at that label has no positive filtration contribution and remains connected to itself, so the empty diagram contributes exactly the single class announced in the Statement.','Statement and Proof3.1')])
add('lem-hecke-intertwiners-produce-local-klr-generators',[
('one',C,'For a one-strand sector there are no crossing generators. The remaining projector and dot identities and the cyclotomic dot relation are exactly those verified before the braid computation, so the presentation map is determined without a nonexistent adjacent-strand relation.','Statement generator ranges; Proof1.1 and2.1'),
('nonempty-choice',C,'The normalizing power series and intertwiners are supplied, and each crossing is the displayed finite sum over content projectors. The presentation universal property determines the homomorphism uniquely from those elements; it makes no further selection of algebra maps.','Given; Proof2.1'),
('iff-forward',NA,'Verifying the cyclotomic KLR relations produces a homomorphism into the Hecke sector. The lemma does not characterize all Hecke sectors by such homomorphisms or assert an algebra isomorphism as a biconditional here.','Statement homomorphism conclusion; Proof2.1'),
('iff-reverse',NA,'The construction sends presentation generators to explicit normalized intertwiners. A converse identifying the source from the existence of that map belongs to the separate bridge, not to this relation-verification lemma.','Statement and Remarks inverse direction of bridge'),
('endpoints',NA,'Dot and crossing subscripts range over the fixed strand positions; no continuous parameter limit or asymptotic strand-count boundary is asserted. The proof checks the local presentation relations in that fixed sector.','Statement and Proof1.1–1.2'),
('zero',C,'The central content sector may vanish. Every constructed term is supported on its content projector, so all generators in the zero sector vanish and the relations and unique presentation map remain meaningful. The conclusion explicitly includes this sector.','Statement possibly zero; Proof3.1'),
('degenerate',C,'The additive Hecke presentation uses its additive intertwiners and normalizing series, whereas the multiplicative presentation uses the corresponding multiplicative ones. The proof checks the dot-sliding and quadratic normalization separately in these supplied branches before invoking the common KLR presentation.','Statement two displayed formulas; Proof1.1–1.2')])
